# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[work]] as a [[lawyer]]; to [[practice]] [[#Noun|law]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|title=New York Court of Appeals. Records and Briefs |author=New York (State). Court of Appeals |page=71 |ISBN= |passage=That was in 1877 you were '''lawing''' with Herdick? }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|title=The Scroll of Phi Delta Theta - Volume 21  |author= |page= 210|ISBN= |passage=J. H. Turner is married and '''lawing''' in Milwaukee. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1923 |title=Time - Volume 29  |author=Briton Hadden  |page=59 |ISBN= |passage=The American Bar Association ruefully admits that the legal profession is overcrowded, especially in large cities. It has a committee studying the situation. Last week an editorial in the New York Law Journal urged a youthful revolt against the city, twanged an idyll of '''lawing''' in the country. }}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|chiefly|dialectal}} To [[prosecute]] or [[sue]] (someone), to [[litigate]].
#* '''1860''', George Eliot (Mary Anne Lewes), ''The Mill on the Floss'':
#*: Your husband's [...] so given to '''lawing''', they say. I doubt he'll leave you poorly off when he dies.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|author=Charles Dudley Warner|title=Their Pilgrimage|page=144|passage="I like folks to be up and down and square," she began saying, as she vigilantly watched the effect of her culinary skill upon the awed little party. "Yes, I've got a regular hotel license; you bet I have. There's been folks '''lawed''' in this town for sellin' a meal of victuals and not having one."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014 |title=The Lives of Chang and Eng: Siam's Twins in Nineteenth-Century America |author=Joseph Andrew Orser  |page= |ISBN= |passage=“So I said to her, 'Well, no man ever made anything '''lawing''' with his wife, so, if your mind is set on having a divorce and the children you will want plenty to raise them with,' so I deed her the farm in Sumner county and everything on it—horses, mules, machinery, everything.” }}
# {{lb|en|nonstandard}} To [[rule]] over (with a certain effect) by [[#Noun|law]]; to [[govern]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1939|author=Henry Green Hodges|title=City management: theory and practice of municipal administration
|passage=At its 1933 session, the Kansas legislature provided for funding outstanding bills and floating debts of those cities which could not make payment by a fixed date. By this stroke of its imagination, the legislature '''lawed''' all Kansas cities onto a "cash" basis and admonished them to stay there.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969|title=Aryan Path - Volume 40  |author= |page=338 |ISBN= |passage=Earth lies in the chorus of the stars' congregation in the '''lawed''' line of their movement, in the balanced rotation of their light, bound by that '''lawed''' line, conceived in the focus of that turning; a vessel fashioned on the wheel of endless time.  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|title=Surat In The Seventeenth Century  |author=Gokhale |page=27 |ISBN=8171542204 |passage=Nicholas Downton (February 1615) says of the people of Surat: "a mixt people, quiet, peaceable, very subtle; civil, and universally governed under one King, but diversely '''lawed''' and customed".  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Henry Grenryk Ledesma|title=The Little Book: The Sound of the Seventh Trumpet|page=38|passage=So that, when GOD said, “Let there be light:” Behold the first created light burst out unto its glory (here GOD '''lawed''' the power of heat, fire, light, melting, cooling, and freezing)}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|title=The Iron Cage |author=Brian Freemantle  |page= |ISBN=1453226672 |passage=Beyond the ocher and yellow-washed buildings, French colonial with a suggestion of Beau Geste from the castellated balconies, it is an arm-grabbing, loosely '''lawed''' bazaar of a place.  }}
# {{lb|en|informal}} To [[enforce]] the [[#Noun|law]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|title=E.K. Means  |author=Eldred Kurtz Means  |page=50 |ISBN= |passage=De gram jury '''lawed''' me all de time an' dat place got too hot. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972 |title=Coaltown revisited: an Appalachian notebook  |author=Bill Peterson  |page=28 |ISBN= |passage=The only time I ever got '''lawed''' [arrested] was for the union. Happened three times. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008 |title=Art in America: A Novel |author=Ron McLarty  |page= |ISBN=1440629935 |passage=So we're on the road with the micks, maybe a mile from the precinct, and Reedy just pulls over, takes them out onto the Commons, takes off the cuffs, and we knock about twenty pounds of shit out of them.” Petey sensed the agent watching him talk and tried to explain it all another way. “What I mean is, '''lawing''' used to be pretty damn pure. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013 |title=Hyde's Corner - Book II - In The Name of Vengeance |author=J B Bergstad |page= |ISBN=0988806835 |passage=The sheriff jabbed his thumb at his chest. "I run this shebang. Been doing so for forty-six years. You think you can come in here and preach '''lawing''' to me? }}
# To [[subject]] to [[legal]] [[restriction]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|title=The Chronicle - Volumes 55-56  |author= |page=125 |ISBN= |passage=Insurance may fairly be said to head the list of objects of legislative interference. It has been '''lawed''' and '''lawed''' until it is nearly outlawed, and the cry for more continues to go up unsatisfied }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|title=California Outlook - Volume 16  |author= |page=lxx |ISBN= |passage=No man knew what his water rights were until they had been '''lawed''' over, and '''lawed''' over, and '''lawed''' over again. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|title=Weight and Measure  |author= |page=34 |ISBN= |passage=It has been truly said that we are '''lawed''' into existence and '''lawed''' through life and '''lawed''' out of it more than any other nation  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|title=The Unbeliever  |author=Lisa Lewis  |page=58 |ISBN=0299144046 |passage=She knows what's tethered underwater. Not Children's bodies, but their toys, their lost, '''Lawed'''-against pleasures }}

